---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { absolute } from "../lib/routes";
import { buildAboutPage } from "../lib/seo.mjs";
import { getPageSeo } from "../lib/supporting-metadata";

const description = getPageSeo("/about").description;
const structuredData = buildAboutPage({
  siteUrl: absolute("/"),
  url: absolute("/about"),
  name: "About Agent Skills",
  description,
  breadcrumbs: [
    { name: "Catalog", url: absolute("/") },
    { name: "About", url: absolute("/about") },
  ],
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/about"
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <article class="prose about-page">
    <h1>About Agent Skills</h1>
    <p>
      An Agent Skill is a folder of instructions that an AI agent loads on
      demand. It holds a <code>SKILL.md</code> file — YAML frontmatter plus
      markdown steps — and optional supporting files. This catalog publishes
      such skills for anyone to read, download, and install.
    </p>

    <h2>Which agents can use these skills?</h2>
    <p>
      Any agent that supports the Agent Skills format: public ChatGPT, Claude,
      Cursor, Claude Code, and other skill-capable tools. The format is plain
      files on disk, so support depends on the agent, not on this catalog.
    </p>

    <h2>How do I install a skill?</h2>
    <p>Pick whichever fits your agent:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Skills CLI.</strong> Run the install command shown on the skill
        page — for example:
        <pre tabindex="0"><code>npx skills add https://skills.merze.ai --skill &lt;name&gt;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Download the files.</strong> Put every listed file in a folder
        named after the skill slug inside your agent's skills directory, then
        reload the agent. The skill page offers a copyable <code>curl</code>
        command that recreates the folder for you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Chat agents.</strong> Some agents cannot read local folders.
        The skill page builds a paste with direct links to
        <code>SKILL.md</code> and each supporting file, which you can hand to
        the agent in one message.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is in a skill folder?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A folder named with the skill slug — lowercase letters, digits, and
        hyphens, 64 characters at most.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>SKILL.md</code> with required frontmatter:
        <ul>
          <li><code>name</code> — must match the folder name</li>
          <li><code>description</code> — 1–1024 characters</li>
          <li>
            Optional <code>metadata.version</code>, <code>source</code>,
            <code>category</code>, <code>tags</code>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        Optional supporting text — markdown, plain text, JSON, YAML, or CSV —
        published beside <code>SKILL.md</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How is this catalog built?</h2>
    <p>
      The site rebuilds from every top-level <code>*/SKILL.md</code> in the
      repository. An invalid <code>name</code> or <code>description</code>, a
      name/folder mismatch, or a disallowed file type fails the build, so a
      broken skill never reaches the catalog.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every skill page shows its own version and last-updated date. The fee
      figures in a skill's supporting files are dated inside those files, with
      links to the official source for each one.
    </p>

    <h2>Why publish them openly?</h2>
    <p>
      A skill is only useful if you can see what it will tell your agent. Plain
      files, a readable version number, and a public download link make a skill
      reviewable before it runs — and easy to update when the rules inside it
      change.
    </p>

    <h2>Who publishes these skills?</h2>
    <p>
      Skills are published by <a href="https://merze.ai">Merze</a>, which also
      offers US company formation, registered-agent, and tax-filing services.
      Publishing a skill is not a recommendation of those services: a skill
      states its own scope and sources, and general information in it is not
      legal, tax, or formation advice.
    </p>
    <p>
      For contributions or questions, contact Merze through
      <a href="https://merze.ai">merze.ai</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
